# ROOT-ANCHORED, and the anchor is load-bearing rather than tidy. These name the
# SDK's `ArtifactsPath` output folder, which is at the repository root. Left
# unanchored, `artifacts/` matches a directory of that name at ANY depth -- and
# on case-insensitive Windows it matched `src/BrowserAI/Artifacts/`, so five
# product source files were invisible to git while every other signal read
# healthy. Caught 2026-08-16, at build-order step 14, by `git check-ignore`
# rather than by anything the build does.
#
# !! THIS COMMENT IS NOW THE ONLY THING GUARDING IT, AND A COMMENT IS NOT A
# MECHANISM. Corrected 2026-08-19 (previously "it is now
# `BuildConfigurationTests.NoSourceFileIsInvisibleToGit`, because a comment is
# not a mechanism"). That test was DELETED on 2026-08-19, deliberately, by the
# maintainer -- not lost in a refactor and not an oversight. The reasoning is in
# QUESTIONS.md, judgement call E. What went with it: nothing now compares the
# `.cs` files on disk under `src/` and `tests/` against `git ls-files`, so a
# source file swallowed by an ignore rule is invisible again, exactly as it was
# on 2026-08-15, and `git status --porcelain` still reports clean because an
# ignored file is not untracked. 64 unanchored directory rules remain in the
# upstream half above -- the predicate is "a line above the BrowserAI marker
# that ends in `/`, does not begin with `/` and is not a negation", re-counted
# 2026-08-19; only these two are anchored. `[Ll]og/`, `[Oo]ut/`, `[Rr]elease/`
# and `[Oo]bj/` are the ones a .NET source folder could realistically collide
# with on case-insensitive Windows.
/artifacts/
/.artifacts/

