###############################################################################
# BrowserAI
#
# Everything above this line is github/gitignore's VisualStudio.gitignore as of
# 2026-04-17 -- re-fetched and compared 2026-08-19 against upstream HEAD
# (VisualStudio.gitignore blob d5a18de, last touched by commit 61268c1e on
# 2026-04-17), which has not moved since.
#
# !! IT IS NOT VERBATIM, AND THE ONE DEVIATION IS LOAD-BEARING. Corrected
# 2026-08-19 (previously "verbatim as of 2026-04-17, so the upstream half can be
# refreshed wholesale"). Upstream's `artifacts/` and `.artifacts/` are
# ROOT-ANCHORED here, with the reason beside them at the line itself. Refreshing
# this half wholesale, which is exactly what that sentence invited, silently
# reverts that -- and unanchored, `artifacts/` matched `src/BrowserAI/Artifacts/`
# on case-insensitive Windows and made five product source files invisible to git
# while the build, the suite and `git status --porcelain` all read green.
#
# So the refresh procedure is: paste, then RE-APPLY the anchoring, then run
# `git check-ignore -v` over `src/` and `tests/` BY HAND. Corrected 2026-08-19
# (previously "then run the suite.
# `BuildConfigurationTests.NoSourceFileIsInvisibleToGit` is what makes forgetting
# a red build rather than a silent loss"). That test was deleted on 2026-08-19 --
# the maintainer's decision, recorded as judgement call E in QUESTIONS.md -- so
# forgetting the re-anchor is a silent loss again and the suite will stay green
# through it. This comment is all there is.
#
# Everything below is project-specific.
#
# Patterns here are anchored with a leading slash wherever a bare directory
# name could collide with a source folder -- Git honours core.ignorecase on
# Windows, so an unanchored `node/` or `mcp/` would also swallow `Node/` and
# `Mcp/` anywhere in the tree.
###############################################################################

# --- Workspace MCP registration ----------------------------------------------
# `.vscode/*` above ignores everything the upstream half does not re-admit, and
# `mcp.json` is not on its list. For a project that IS an MCP server, a workspace
# registration used for testing would therefore be silently untracked -- the
# failure mode this file's own header is about.
#
# Upstream proposes fixing it: github/gitignore#4735, open since 2025-09-23, last
# touched 2025-07-24, still unmerged when checked 2026-08-19. Re-admitted here in
# the meantime, below the marker, so a wholesale refresh of the upstream half
# cannot drop it. If that PR merges, this line becomes redundant rather than
# wrong, and the upstream half's own negation takes over.
!.vscode/mcp.json

# --- Bundled payload ---------------------------------------------------------
# The installer payload (node.exe ~88 MB + the @playwright/mcp JS tree ~18 MB +
# BrowserAI itself) is assembled at build time and never committed. Browsers are
# NOT in it -- they are provisioned on first run, so no Chromium tree is ever
# staged here. See PLAN.md section A.
#
# Observed 2026-08-16, build-order step 3: build/Build-Payload.ps1 assembles
# exactly one of these three, /payload/, laid out as
#
#   payload/node/{node.exe,LICENSE}   payload/mcp/node_modules/...
#   payload/.cache/node-<ver>-win-x64.zip   payload/payload.json
#
# so /payload/ is now a real path rather than a guess.
#
# /staging/ and /.staging/ are GONE, deleted 2026-08-16 at build-order step 19.
# They were kept "as spare conventions" on the theory that packaging would claim
# one; packaging landed and claimed neither. The release script publishes to
# /artifacts/ (the SDK's own folder, already ignored above) and packs into
# /Releases/. An ignore rule for a path nothing emits is a rule that can only
# ever hide something nobody expected.
#
# The tracked half lives at build/payload/ -- package.json and the resolved
# package-lock.json -- and is deliberately OUTSIDE this rule: the lock is the
# provenance stamp and must be committed.
#
# Verified 2026-08-15: vpk emits none of these three.
/payload/

# playwright-core writes .links/ into the BROWSERS root -- never into
# node_modules, measured 2026-08-16 (kb: first-run provisioning). Browsers are
# provisioned on first run and live outside the repository entirely, so this
# rule has nothing to match today. Kept as a cheap backstop against an upstream
# that moves it into the package tree, which is the only way it could ever
# reach a commit.
.links/

# node_modules/ is already ignored by the upstream template (~line 316), which
# is what keeps the assembled tree out of history. If it is ever committed
# instead, for reproducibility, negate it -- note the real path:
#   !/payload/mcp/node_modules/
# and remember that Git does not descend into an excluded directory, so
# /payload/ itself has to be negated back on first.

# --- Velopack ----------------------------------------------------------------
# *.nupkg (full and delta packages) and *.msi come from the upstream template.
#
# Verified 2026-08-15 against a real `vpk pack`, which emits into Releases/:
#   {id}-{version}-full.nupkg   {id}-{version}-delta.nupkg
#   {id}-{channel}-Setup.exe    {id}-{channel}-Portable.zip
#   releases.{channel}.json     assets.{channel}.json     RELEASES
# vpk does NOT prune: after five versions all five fulls and four deltas remain.
/Releases/
/releases/
/RELEASES
# Bare `Setup.exe` never matches -- the real name carries the app id and channel.
*-Setup.exe
Setup.exe.log
*-Portable.zip
# Feed manifests, emitted beside the packages.
releases.*.json
assets.*.json

# --- Playwright / MCP run output ---------------------------------------------
.playwright-mcp/
trace.zip
*.trace.zip

# --- Test output -------------------------------------------------------------
# *.received.* is already ignored above by the Approval Tests rule, which is
# exactly what Verify.TUnit needs. *.verified.* is deliberately NOT ignored:
# approved snapshots are committed.
TestResults/
*.trx

# --- Agent scratch space -----------------------------------------------------
# CLAUDE.md, "Scratch work": agent-produced temporary files go here. In practice
# that means multi-gigabyte browser trees and probe output, so leaving it
# untracked rather than ignored puts the whole lot one `git add -A` away from
# history. Deliberately not slash-anchored, unlike the paths above -- a leading
# dot already rules out the source-folder collision this file's header warns
# about, and every other repo in the estate writes it bare.
.work/

# --- Claude Code local state -------------------------------------------------
# Deny-by-default, then re-admit the two shared files. Ignoring only
# settings.local.json is the older habit here and it leaks: runtime state
# (session locks, MCP approval caches) is not covered by that one name and
# reaches a commit. `hooks/` must be negated as a directory -- Git does not
# descend into an excluded one, so a file inside cannot be negated back on
# its own.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json
!.claude/hooks/

# --- Secrets: certificates, keys and signing material ------------------------
# Matched by artifact class, never by filename. The evidence is from this
# estate: a repository ignored `key.properties` -- the passwords file -- and
# carried no `*.jks` rule at all, so the release-signing keystore those
# passwords protected sat committed while the ignore rules read as complete
# cover. A filename list is only ever as good as the names someone thought of;
# an extension class does not depend on anyone having thought of them.
#
# `*.pfx` already appears in the upstream half above, and `*.snk` is there
# commented out. Both are asserted again here on purpose: this half is the one
# that gets read, and re-fetching the upstream half wholesale (TODO.md) must not
# be able to quietly drop either. `.gitattributes` already calls most of these
# binary -- that suppresses a diff, not a commit.
#
# Nothing in this project needs a committed certificate today. If a test fixture
# ever does, negate it by exact path (`!tests/Fixtures/localhost.pfx`), so the
# exception is visible in the diff rather than implied by a missing rule.

# Key stores and certificate containers.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pfx
*.p7b
*.pem
*.der
*.cer
*.crt

# Private keys and signing material, including the extensionless OpenSSH forms.
*.key
*.snk
*.pk8
*.p8
*.ppk
*.gpg
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
*_rsa
*_ed25519

# --- BrowserAI session directories -------------------------------------------
# BrowserAI requires an explicit session directory and accepts any path the
# caller names (DECISIONS.md, "Shape and packaging"), so a session driven
# repository during development -- which will happen -- lands its tree wherever
# it was pointed. There is no path to anchor to, only names. These are the real
# names, taken from the plan's session-directory and artifact-management
# sections rather than guessed:
#
#   <session-dir>\  lock.json  session.json  profile\  downloads\
#                   output\{console,network,page,request,response,result,
#                           storage-state,video,traces}\
#
# `storage-state` is the one that matters most: those files are live session
# bearer tokens, including the httpOnly cookies JavaScript cannot read, so it is
# matched as a loose name-class too and not only as a folder. The per-session
# log sits beside `lock.json` and is already covered by the upstream half's
# `*.log`.
#
# `lock.json` is a whole-name match: it does not touch `packages.lock.json`,
# which must stay tracked because the floating-dependency check diffs it.
#
# The typed folders carry their `output/` parent so a future `src\...\Page\` or
# `Result\` is not swallowed -- core.ignorecase applies, per this section's
# header. `profile/` and `downloads/` sit at the session root and cannot be
# scoped that way; if this repository ever grows source folders by those names,
# negate them here.
lock.json
session.json
storage-state*
profile/
downloads/
**/output/console/
**/output/network/
**/output/page/
**/output/request/
**/output/response/
**/output/result/
**/output/storage-state/
**/output/video/
**/output/traces/
